response:
 This problem is about finding the area of a square garden that has the same perimeter as a rectangular garden with a length of 40 feet and a width of 10 feet. First, we start by calculating the perimeter of the rectangular garden. The perimeter \(P\) of a rectangle is given by: \[
P = 2 \times (\text{length} + \text{width}) = 2 \times (40 + 10) = 2 \times 50 = 100 \text{ feet}
\] Since the new garden is a square and it uses the same perimeter, the perimeter of the square is also 100 feet. Let the side length of the square be \(s\). The perimeter of the square is given by: \[
P = 4 \times s = 100 \implies s = \frac{100}{4} = 25 \text{ feet}
\] The area of the rectangular garden is: \[
\text{Area}_{\text{rectangle}} = \text{length} \times \text{width} = 40 \times 10 = 400 \text{ square feet}
\] The area of the square garden is: \[
\text{Area}_{\text{square}} = s^2 = 25^2 = 625 \text{ square feet}
\] The difference in area between the new square garden and the old rectangular garden is: \[
\Delta \text{Area} = \text{Area}_{\text{square}} - \text{Area}_{\text{rectangle}} = 625 - 400 = 225 \text{ square feet}
\]
